{"name":"Twilight Bloom","hex":"#cfb4c2","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"twilight","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The soft light in the sky seen before the rising and (especially) after the setting of the sun, occasioned by the illumination of the earth’s atmosphere by the direct rays of the sun and their reflection on the earth.","The time when said light is visible; the period between daylight and darkness."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Pertaining to or resembling twilight; faintly illuminated; obscure.","Synonym of mesopelagic (“Describing the pelagic zone of the ocean between the photic epipelagic and the aphotic bathypelagic zones, characterized by very minimal light.”)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/twilight"},{"term":"bloom","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A blossom; the flower of a plant; an expanded bud.","Flowers."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cause to blossom; to make flourish.","To bestow a bloom upon; to make blooming or radiant."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bloom"}],"entries":[{"title":"List of Advanced Dungeons & Dragons 2nd edition monsters","blocks":[{"text":"Monsters are an important element of Advanced Dungeons & Dragons 2nd edition, a role-playing game. The ones listed here are only those from official Advanced Dungeons & Dragons 2nd Edition supplements published by TSR, Inc. or Wizards of the Coast, not licensed or unlicensed third-party products such as video games or unlicensed Advanced Dungeons & Dragons 2nd Edition manuals."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Dados_4_a_20_caras_trans.png/330px-Dados_4_a_20_caras_trans.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Advanced_Dungeons_%26_Dragons_2nd_edition_monsters"},{"title":"Bloom (shader effect)","blocks":[{"text":"Bloom is a computer graphics effect used in video games, demos, and high-dynamic-range rendering (HDRR) to reproduce an imaging artifact of real-world cameras. The effect produces fringes of light extending from the borders of bright areas in an image, contributing to the illusion of an extremely bright light overwhelming the camera or eye capturing the scene. It became widely used in video games after an article on the technique was published by the authors of Tron 2.0 in 2004."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Elephants_Dream_-_Emo_and_Proog.png/330px-Elephants_Dream_-_Emo_and_Proog.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bloom_(shader_effect)"},{"title":"Twilight","blocks":[{"text":"Twilight is daylight illumination produced by diffuse sky radiation when the Sun is below the horizon as sunlight from the upper atmosphere is scattered in a way that illuminates both the Earth's lower atmosphere and also the Earth's surface. Twilight also may be any period when this illumination occurs, including dawn and dusk."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Twilight_description_full_day.svg/330px-Twilight_description_full_day.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twilight"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9a/HrdiBloomExample.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9a/HrdiBloomExample.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"An example of bloom in a picture taken with a camera. Note the blue fringe that is particularly noticeable along the right edge of the window.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHrdiBloomExample.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Blooming_ccd.jpg/500px-Blooming_ccd.jpg?utm